## Theorem 3.5 -- Squeeze theorem

If $a_n \leq b_n \leq c_n$ for all $n$, and $a_n \to L$ and $c_n \to L$,
then $b_n \to L$.

**Proof idea:** For any $\epsilon > 0$, choose $N_1$ and $N_2$ so that $|a_n - L| < \epsilon$ and
$|c_n - L| < \epsilon$ for $n \geq \max(N_1, N_2)$. Then $L - \epsilon < a_n \leq b_n \leq c_n < L + \epsilon$,
so $|b_n - L| < \epsilon$.

```coq
Lemma ex_3_5 (a b c : ℕ → ℝ) (L : ℝ) :
    a ⟶ L → c ⟶ L →
    (∀ n ∈ ℕ, a n ≤ b n) →
    (∀ n ∈ ℕ, b n ≤ c n) →
    b ⟶ L.
Proof.
  Assume that a ⟶ L as (Ha).
  Assume that c ⟶ L as (Hc).
  Assume that ∀ n ∈ ℕ, a n ≤ b n as (Hab).
  Assume that ∀ n ∈ ℕ, b n ≤ c n as (Hbc).
  (* Useful exercise: delete the following line and prove
     the lemma in details as done above *)
  By squeeze_theorem we conclude that b ⟶ L.
Qed.
```

